  "i": [
   {
    "id": "i1",
    "kind": "Javadoc",
    "lines": [
     15,
     24
    ],
    "own_line": true,
    "norm": "Tests {@link StringSubstitutor}'s handling of complex escaping. <p>The escape character is {@code $}. Writing it twice ({@code $$}) emits a single literal {@code $} into the output without triggering substitution. Combined with the default {@code ${...}} variable syntax this lets a template produce literal {@code ${...}} text while still substituting nested variables. For example, the template {@code $${${a}}} first resolves the inner variable {@code ${a}} to its value {@code 1}, then the leading {@code $$} escape turns the surrounding {@code ${...}} into the literal text {@code ${1}}.</p>"
   },
   {
    "id": "i2",
    "kind": "Javadoc",
    "lines": [
     31,
     31
    ],
    "own_line": true,
    "norm": "Variable name to value mappings shared by every substitution in this test."
   },
   {
    "id": "i3",
    "kind": "LineComment",
    "lines": [
     37,
     38
    ],
    "own_line": true,
    "norm": "Single-character keys/values and short repeated variants, used to verify escaping is independent of variable name/value length."
   },
   {
    "id": "i4",
    "kind": "LineComment",
    "lines": [
     46,
     46
    ],
    "own_line": true,
    "norm": "Longer, more realistic variables."
   },
   {
    "id": "i5",
    "kind": "Javadoc",
    "lines": [
     51,
     57
    ],
    "own_line": true,
    "norm": "Tests complex escaping: each template is expected to resolve to the given literal result. <p>The {@code substring} flag indicates that the same substitution should additionally be verified when applied to the inner part of the template (the template minus its first and last characters), exercising the offset/length overloads of {@code replace}.</p>"
   },
   {
    "id": "i6",
    "kind": "LineComment",
    "lines": [
     60,
     61
    ],
    "own_line": true,
    "norm": "\"$$\" escapes the leading \"$\" so the outer \"${...}\" survives as literal text, while the inner variable is still substituted."
   },
   {
    "id": "i7",
    "kind": "LineComment",
    "lines": [
     69,
     70
    ],
    "own_line": true,
    "norm": "Both \"$\" characters of the inner \"${...}\" are escaped, so nothing inside is substituted and the whole inner expression is emitted literally."
   },
   {
    "id": "i8",
    "kind": "LineComment",
    "lines": [
     76,
     76
    ],
    "own_line": true,
    "norm": "Surrounding literal text (leading/trailing dots) is preserved verbatim."
   },
   {
    "id": "i9",
    "kind": "LineComment",
    "lines": [
     83,
     84
    ],
    "own_line": true,
    "norm": "The \"-\" default-value syntax: \"undefined.number\" is unknown, so its default \"1234567890\" is used, then escaped into literal \"${1234567890}\"."
   },
   {
    "id": "i10",
    "kind": "Javadoc",
    "lines": [
     89,
     98
    ],
    "own_line": true,
    "norm": "Substitutes {@code template} with a {@link StringSubstitutor} backed by {@link #values} and asserts the result equals {@code expectedResult} across every {@code replace}/{@code replaceIn} overload {@link StringSubstitutor} offers. @param expectedResult the expected substitution result for the full template @param template the template to substitute @param substring whether to additionally verify the offset/length overloads against the template's inner portion (first and last characters removed)"
   },
